{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n41 \n \n \nphotographs may be requested from the Project Scientist, Dennis Stocker \n(dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov). \n \n2. Video \nAn example video from the Flame Design experiment can be found with the web-\nbased article at: \nhttps://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/station/research/news/combustion-\nresearch-microgravity-clean-burning-fuel-space-station/. \n \n\u2018The Power of Studying Combustion on the ISS,\u2019 which was developed with input \nfrom the ACME Project Scientist, is available at https://science.nasa.gov/science-\nnews/news-articles/the-power-of-studying-combustion-on-the-iss. \n \nVideo(s) describing the ACME project can be found at: \nhttps://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/. \n \nVideos related to Flame Design and other microgravity combustion research can \nalso be seen on Facebook at www.facebook.com/space.flames. \n  \nContact Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov) for access to those videos or \nfor additional videos. \n \n \nIV. Additional Comments from science team \nConstantly sooting flames were only observed in 40% oxygen and high fuel concentrations.  In \n21% oxygen, small flames did not soot because of heat loss to the burner and large flames did \nnot soot because high radiative losses caused the flame temperature to quickly drop below the \nsooting limit.  Preliminary analysis of the HiBMs images suggests that significant emission of light \nfrom soot particles and dissymmetry of the soot cloud will make acquiring accurate soot volume \nfraction measurements difficult, if possible. The cloud of soot particles also shows relatively \nextreme dissymmetry, further complicating our ability to obtain soot volume fraction \nmeasurements.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 41}}